CAT = "arrtmriPhys"; SEG = "PHY"
G = 42.58
assert round(G * 1.5, 1) == 63.9
assert round(G * 3.0, 1) == 127.7
assert round(G * 0.5, 1) == 21.3
assert round(G * 1.0, 2) == 42.58
_cs = 220 / 110; assert _cs == 2
_cs3 = 440 / 110; assert _cs3 == 4
_f = 21.29 / G; assert abs(_f - 0.5) < 0.001
Q = [
("Which law explains why a changing magnetic field near a coil induces a voltage, the basis of the MR signal?",
 "Faraday's law", ["Ohm's law of resistance", "The inverse square law", "Coulomb's law of charges"],
 "Faraday's law says a changing magnetic flux through a conductor induces a voltage. The precessing magnetization does this in the receive coil."),
("What keeps the windings of a superconducting MRI magnet cold enough to carry current with no resistance?",
 "Liquid helium", ["Liquid nitrogen", "Chilled water", "Forced cold air"],
 "Niobium-titanium windings are bathed in liquid helium at about 4 kelvin, where they superconduct. Chilled water cools the gradients and electronics."),
("Which magnet type needs no electricity or cryogen to keep its field, but is very heavy and limited to low field strengths?",
 "Permanent magnet", ["Superconducting magnet", "Resistive electromagnet", "Hybrid cryogen-free magnet"],
 "A permanent magnet uses ferromagnetic material and holds its field without power, but its weight limits it to low fields, usually well under 1 T."),
("Which magnet's field can be switched off quickly by cutting the current, with no quench?",
 "Resistive electromagnet", ["Superconducting magnet", "Permanent magnet", "Niobium-tin magnet"],
 "A resistive magnet needs constant current and produces heat, so turning off the current removes the field. A permanent magnet cannot be switched off, and a superconducting one needs a ramp-down or quench."),
("What is the job of the shim coils?",
 "Make the main field more uniform", ["Transmit the RF pulse", "Encode position along the phase axis", "Cool the gradient windings"],
 "Shimming corrects small field inhomogeneities. A uniform field is needed for fat suppression, EPI and spectroscopy."),
("Compared with a large body coil, why does a small surface coil give a higher SNR over the area it covers?",
 "It sits close and gathers less noise", ["It transmits a stronger and longer RF pulse", "It has a wider receive bandwidth", "It covers the whole body evenly"],
 "A small receive coil placed close to the anatomy collects signal from nearby tissue and noise from only a small volume. Its sensitivity falls off with depth."),
("Several small receive coils whose signals are combined, with the coverage of a large coil and the SNR of small ones, are called what?",
 "A phased array coil", ["A quadrature body coil", "A single-loop surface coil", "A gradient coil set"],
 "A phased array coil has many elements, each with its own receiver channel. The elements also make parallel imaging possible."),
("What does gradient slew rate describe?",
 "Gradient amplitude over rise time", ["How long the RF pulse lasts", "The share of time a gradient can run", "The width of the transmit bandwidth"],
 "Slew rate (mT/m/ms) is how fast a gradient reaches its peak amplitude. A higher slew rate allows shorter TE and TR and faster sequences such as EPI."),
("What does a gradient's duty cycle limit?",
 "Time it can spend at high power", ["The number of receive coil elements", "The highest static field strength", "The diameter of the patient bore"],
 "Duty cycle is the percentage of time the gradient system can operate at a given power without overheating. Demanding sequences such as DWI use a large share of it."),
("What is the Larmor frequency of hydrogen at 1.5 T?",
 "About 63.9 MHz", ["About 42.6 MHz", "About 127.7 MHz", "About 21.3 MHz"],
 "The Larmor equation is frequency = gyromagnetic ratio x field strength. For hydrogen, 42.58 MHz/T x 1.5 T = about 63.9 MHz."),
("What is the precession frequency of hydrogen protons at 3.0 T?",
 "About 127.7 MHz", ["About 63.9 MHz", "About 255.5 MHz", "About 42.6 MHz"],
 "42.58 MHz/T x 3.0 T = about 127.7 MHz. Doubling the field doubles the frequency."),
("A low-field system's hydrogen frequency is about 21.3 MHz. What is its field strength?",
 "0.5 T", ["1.0 T", "0.2 T", "1.5 T"],
 "Field = frequency / gyromagnetic ratio = 21.29 / 42.58 = about 0.5 T."),
("In the Larmor equation, what is the gyromagnetic ratio?",
 "A constant for each nucleus", ["The field strength of the magnet", "The flip angle of the RF pulse", "A value the technologist sets"],
 "The gyromagnetic ratio is a property of each nucleus (42.58 MHz/T for hydrogen). It does not change with the scanner or the protocol."),
("At equilibrium in the main field, how is the net magnetization of the patient oriented?",
 "Along the main field", ["Across the main field, in the transverse plane", "At 45 degrees to the main field", "With no net direction at all"],
 "Slightly more protons sit in the low-energy state, aligned with the field, so the net magnetization points along B0. It gives no signal until an RF pulse tips it."),
("Why must the RF pulse be sent at the Larmor frequency?",
 "Protons absorb only at that frequency", ["Other frequencies would set off a quench", "The gradient coils need that frequency", "It keeps the SAR at exactly zero"],
 "Resonance means energy transfers efficiently only when the pulse matches the precession frequency. That is also how slice selection picks out one slab."),
("Right after a 90-degree RF pulse, where is the net magnetization?",
 "Fully in the transverse plane", ["Pointing against the main field", "Unchanged along the main field", "Split evenly between both axes"],
 "A 90-degree pulse tips the net magnetization into the transverse plane, where it induces signal. A 180-degree pulse inverts it."),
("Besides tipping the magnetization, what does the RF pulse do to the protons' spins?",
 "Puts them into phase with one another", ["Stops their precession", "Changes their gyromagnetic ratio", "Lines them up against the field forever"],
 "The pulse brings spins into phase coherence, so their transverse components add up to a measurable signal. Loss of that coherence is T2 decay."),
("What is the free induction decay (FID)?",
 "The signal decaying after the pulse ends", ["The echo formed by a 180-degree pulse", "A gradient-induced current in the patient", "The noise floor of an empty receive coil"],
 "When the RF pulse stops, the transverse magnetization induces a signal that decays quickly as spins dephase. Spin echo sequences refocus it into an echo."),
("Gadolinium shortens relaxation times because of which kind of magnetism?",
 "Paramagnetism", ["Diamagnetism", "Ferromagnetism", "Superconductivity"],
 "Gadolinium's unpaired electrons make it strongly paramagnetic, which speeds T1 recovery of nearby water protons, so enhancing tissue turns bright on T1-weighted images."),
("Most body tissue and water are weakly repelled by a magnetic field. What is this property called?",
 "Diamagnetism", ["Paramagnetism", "Superparamagnetism", "Ferromagnetism"],
 "Diamagnetic materials have no unpaired electrons and slightly oppose the applied field. Ferromagnetic materials are strongly attracted and pose projectile risk."),
("Iron oxide particles used as contrast are strongly magnetized in a field but keep no magnetism outside it. Which term describes them?",
 "Superparamagnetic", ["Ferromagnetic", "Diamagnetic", "Nonmagnetic"],
 "Superparamagnetic iron oxide is more strongly magnetized than paramagnetic agents but, unlike ferromagnetic iron, loses its magnetization when the field is removed. It causes signal loss on T2 and T2*."),
("By the end of one T1 time constant, how much of its longitudinal magnetization has a tissue recovered?",
 "About 63%", ["About 37%", "About 50%", "About 90%"],
 "T1 is defined as the time for longitudinal magnetization to regrow to 63% of its full value after a 90-degree pulse."),
("After one T2 time constant, how much transverse magnetization remains?",
 "About 37%", ["About 63%", "About 50%", "About 10%"],
 "T2 is the time for transverse magnetization to decay to 37% of its starting value, as spins lose phase through spin-spin interactions."),
("Why does T2* decay happen faster than true T2 decay?",
 "Field unevenness adds dephasing", ["The RF pulse is weaker", "T1 recovery speeds the decay up", "The gradients refocus the spins"],
 "T2* includes dephasing from field inhomogeneities and susceptibility differences as well as spin-spin effects. A 180-degree refocusing pulse removes the fixed inhomogeneity part, which gradient echoes cannot."),
("Which tissue has a short T1 and appears bright on a T1-weighted image?",
 "Fat", ["Cerebrospinal fluid", "Edema", "Urine in the bladder"],
 "Fat recovers longitudinal magnetization quickly, so it is bright on T1. Free water such as CSF has a long T1 and looks dark."),
("What does a proton density image chiefly reflect?",
 "How many mobile protons each voxel holds", ["How fast each tissue recovers its T1", "How fast each tissue loses its phase", "How fast water diffuses through tissue"],
 "Proton density weighting minimizes T1 and T2 effects with a long TR and short TE, so signal follows the concentration of mobile hydrogen."),
("Which gradient is switched on while the RF pulse is sent, so only one slab of tissue resonates?",
 "Slice select gradient", ["Phase-encoding gradient", "Frequency-encoding gradient", "Shim gradient"],
 "The slice select gradient makes the Larmor frequency vary along one axis, so an RF pulse of a given frequency and bandwidth excites only one slice."),
("Which gradient is on while the signal is being sampled?",
 "Readout gradient", ["Slice select gradient", "Phase-encoding gradient", "Spoiler gradient lobe"],
 "The readout gradient makes spins precess at different frequencies along one axis during sampling, so the Fourier transform can place signal by frequency."),
("In a standard 2D acquisition, how many times is the phase-encoding gradient stepped?",
 "Once per phase matrix line", ["Once for the whole scan", "Once for each slice only", "Once for each echo in the readout"],
 "Each TR uses a different phase-encoding amplitude to fill one line of k-space. A 256 phase matrix needs 256 steps (for one average), which is why phase matrix drives scan time."),
("Which part of k-space mainly sets image contrast and signal?",
 "The center lines", ["The outer edges", "The corners only", "The last lines filled"],
 "Low spatial frequencies at the center of k-space hold contrast and most signal. The outer lines hold fine detail and edges."),
("A wrist larger than the field of view appears folded over the other side of the image. What is this artifact?",
 "Aliasing", ["Gibbs ringing", "Chemical shift", "Zipper artifact"],
 "Tissue outside the FOV in the phase direction is assigned a wrong phase and wraps into the image. Larger FOV, phase oversampling or saturation bands fix it."),
("Fine parallel lines appear next to a sharp edge such as the spinal cord boundary. Which change reduces them?",
 "Increase the phase matrix", ["Increase slice thickness", "Lower the flip angle", "Turn off the shim"],
 "Gibbs (truncation) artifact comes from sampling too few lines to describe a sharp edge. More phase encoding steps or filtering reduce it."),
("At 1.5 T, fat and water differ by about 220 Hz. With a receive bandwidth of 110 Hz per pixel, how far is fat shifted along the readout axis?",
 "2 pixels", ["1 pixel", "4 pixels", "0.5 pixel"],
 "Shift in pixels = frequency difference / bandwidth per pixel = 220 / 110 = 2 pixels. Halving the bandwidth per pixel would double the shift."),
("The same protocol moves from 1.5 T to 3 T, with the fat-water difference doubling to about 440 Hz and 110 Hz per pixel kept. What is the shift now?",
 "4 pixels", ["2 pixels", "8 pixels", "1 pixel"],
 "Chemical shift in hertz scales with field strength: 440 / 110 = 4 pixels. Widening the bandwidth per pixel would bring it back down."),
("On an out-of-phase gradient echo image, a dark line outlines the kidneys at fat-water borders in every direction. What is this?",
 "Out-of-phase (chemical misregistration) artifact", ["Chemical shift of the first kind along the readout axis", "Gibbs truncation ringing at sharp edges", "Moire interference fringes at the edges"],
 "When fat and water are out of phase, voxels holding both lose signal, drawing a dark line at every interface. It depends on TE, not on the readout direction."),
("Signal loss and distortion appear around a dental bridge on a gradient echo brain image. Which change helps most?",
 "Use a spin echo sequence", ["Use a longer TE", "Use a smaller matrix in the readout", "Turn off the fat saturation"],
 "Susceptibility artifact is worst on gradient echo because nothing refocuses the dephasing. Spin echo, short TE, higher bandwidth and smaller voxels reduce it."),
("A straight bright line of noise crosses every image at one point along the frequency axis. What is the most likely cause?",
 "Outside RF through an open door", ["Patient breathing during the scan", "A wrong slice gap in the stack", "Too many signal averages set"],
 "A zipper line at one frequency is outside RF entering the room through a gap in the RF shield, often a door left ajar or a damaged seal."),
("When slices are acquired with no gap, signal drops because each pulse partly excites the next slice. What is this called?",
 "Cross-talk", ["Moire pattern", "Aliasing", "Dielectric effect"],
 "Imperfect slice profiles overlap, so neighbouring slices are partly saturated. A gap or interleaved slice order reduces it. Cross-excitation is the term when stacks intersect."),
("At 3 T, a large abdomen shows a dark central shading even though the coil works. Which effect causes it?",
 "Dielectric effect", ["Eddy currents in the coil", "Partial volume averaging", "Truncation of the signal"],
 "At higher field the RF wavelength in tissue is comparable to body size, causing uneven B1 and shading. Dielectric pads and multi-transmit RF help."),
("Which daily quality control check confirms the system's resonant frequency before scanning?",
 "Center frequency check", ["Slice thickness check", "Door seal inspection", "Geometric accuracy check"],
 "The center frequency is checked daily (often with the ACR phantom). A drift can point to a shim problem or field change."),
]
